    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study identifies and steers language-specific neurons, but does not measure whether neuron poles have one or more understandable jobs, test explanations on held-out activations, or assess the other claim versions.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports LAPE-based language-neuron counts and distributions, neuron steering, and downstream task results. These measure language specialization and control, not the interpretability, explanatory accuracy, or circuit sufficiency named by the claim versions. Language-forcing success rates are recorded under fits_no_version; the paper's other neuron counts, plots, and downstream results do not test a claim version.",
    "test_reason": "The study identifies language-specific FFN neurons with LAPE and steers output language by adding or zeroing their activations. It never estimates what share of neurons have one or two understandable jobs, never scores explanations on held-out activations, and never builds attribution graphs. Its only mention of polysemantic neurons is a speculative aside about Hindi/Urdu robustness, and superposition appears only as related-work background. Its results bear on no claim version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Largely correct. Add that the authors report their additive method outperforming replacement and DiffMean baselines on language forcing. Add that steering works better for high-resource languages and reveals fallback-language hierarchies. The limitation about isolated neurons versus distributed subnetworks is stated by the authors.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Uses LAPE to identify language-specific FFN neurons and additive activation steering, rather than evaluating single-job explanations or held-out explanation fidelity.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Language-forcing results vary with model, selected neuron fraction, and whether the strategy activates target-language neurons alone or also deactivates source-language neurons; the text also reports differences by language resource level. Downstream effects vary by task and language.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Additive steering of LAPE-selected language neurons forces output language. Success rates at top 1–5% of neurons are 30.5–55.4% for Llama-3.1-8B, 17.5–33.2% for Mistral-Nemo and 66.4–86.8% for Aya-Expanse-8B. Deactivate+activate beats activate-only in every cell, and additive beats replacement and DiffMean.",
        "proposed_claim": "Steering language-specific neurons shifts output language, with success depending on model, strategy, neuron fraction and language resource level.",
